A||||64||yellowFormula||Why did everyone buy their Fiero(s)?||||7||
Z||000000||yellowFormula||09-16-1999||09:33 PM||||Well, I think everyone liked the thread about describing themselves, so I figured we could have a thread about WHY we bought our Fiero(s). I guess I will have to be first...<P>Well, I bought my Fiero because originally I liked the appearence the most. It was when I started researching the car when I truly found out what is to like (besides appearence). First and foremost I lik the price of both the car and insurance! I also like the gas mileage from the V6, being a student and having a part time job (both pretty far) that is very important to me. I also like the performance potential, the availiblity of parts, the ease of upgrading things. Probably the thing I like most about my Formula (and my coupes but I don't drive them) is the uniqueness and enjoyment of driving it every day.<P><BR>-jeff   <BR>'88 Formula - yellow 5 sp.<BR>'88 Coupe - black, parts car<BR>'87 Coupe - maroon, sisters for now||||reg||7
Z||000001||batboy||09-16-1999||10:41 PM||||I liked the design of the Fiero from day one when it was introduced, but was a poor college grad student back then. Finally got tired of rusty cars, so bought my first Fiero about 3-1/2 years ago. Now I've collected three of them. Something about the Fieros that makes you fall madly in love with them.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Harry||09-16-1999||10:52 PM||||Well I was a die hard Hot rodder in the past I have mostly been into the traditional rods IE; Camaros/Novas/etc.etc. (yes i was one of the many who used to scoff the Fieros).<BR>Being the un-enlighted types.<BR>Well I had decided to sell my Nova while living in the country areas of Ohio.<BR>I was asking 3500 for the car and was getting no responses from people who had the money to pay my price.<BR>One day this kid come up and says would you be willing to trade and work a deal around the price, I said lets look.<BR>He brought me a white 85 GT that seemed to run good but needed alot of suspesion work up front.<BR>I liked the looks of the car and the fact my mileage would be much better, so I went to the local library and did some research into the Fiero. Then I looked on the web.<BR>What I discovered about our amazing little cars impressed me very much. So I called this kid back and told him You give me the Fiero and $1000 in cash and its yours.<BR>He bought, I got bit (by the bug) and well<BR>HELLO MY NAMES HARRY AND IM A FIEROHOLIC!||||reg||7
Z||000003||Cooter||09-16-1999||11:22 PM||||I can't really pin point the time that I decided that I wanted a fiero. I never really thought much about them until about a year ago. Then some mysterious force drew me to a car lot by my parents house and there sat a rather used '86 fastback GT. After negotiating a price, I drove the car and it really sucked! It had terminal problems. But, on the back row sat a lonely looking 85 se with only 60,000 miles on it. It needed a home and ran like a champ, so $1000 later I had a "new" car. Since then, I have added a fastback clip, new paint, shocks etc. . . and have a great car. Because of the hot weather here in Alabama and the fact that none of my brother's cars have working air, I let him borrow the car a month ago and have only seen it once since! I am addicted and am going through withdrawals from not getting my daily shot of Fieromania.||||reg||14
Z||000004||AL||09-17-1999||12:00 AM||||well i have always liked trans am's and still do but the fiero, always looked like my 89 gta but in small version.i always liked the fiero but for some reason this year some thing told me to buy one. i guess i got hooked on the desighn, and the fact that it could kick some rice burners buts, so in closing if we new exactly why, we would of never bought one. 86 gt. i guess we are the special one's and know what others are missing. excitement!||||reg||10
Z||000005||kel||09-17-1999||12:47 AM||||In 1984, when they first came out, I thought it was one of the most beautiful, unique cars I'd ever seen.  Ordered one about 2 years later. (got it at a discount...my husband works for GMAC)  I've never regretted it!    ||||reg||1
Z||000006||G25C||09-17-1999||12:47 AM||||Im a huge Pontiac nut.  When i was 13 back in 87, i was at the local swimming pool.  Everyday i would see a crowd of kids hanging around this small 2 seater.  It was a Toyota MR2 (i feel like i need a shower from typing that).  I looked it over and thought that it was cool and i said to myself, "why dont Pontiac make something like that?"<P>(i had never heard of a Fiero at that time, i was just getting into Firebirds and GTO's)<P>The next day i was at the swimming pool and noticed that all those kids werent around the MR2 anymore, they were around a car i had never seen before, and it looked SO much cooler than a MR2!!!  So i walked around it liking everything i saw, and i said to myself, "damn! this is cool, why the hell dont Pontiac make one of these?'  And thats when i seen the name "PONTIAC" on it...i remember looking atleast 5 times to see if i was seeing things or not.  It was a maroon coupe.<P>Then the i saw the GT's i said i would love to have a new (again, this was 87) GT.  I wanted it black with grey int.  sunroof, wing and all the options.<P>Well, i now have the EXACT fiero i have always wanted! (ok, i dont have a wing, i have the luggage rack.  But that can be changed)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||ggarsk||09-17-1999||01:11 AM||||When I first saw a Fiero I knew That I would own one....it just took longer then expected. Now I own a 84 SE and it is a blast to drive. I know why people like them so much!||||reg||7
Z||000008||GWMORGAN||09-17-1999||01:39 AM||||Sometime back in 1996, I was passed on the freeway by this beautiful red two seater sports car. After struggling to catch up with this car (doing about 90 mph), I saw the words "fiero 2m6" written on the deck lid. I immidiately fell in love with the car.<BR>    Later that day, I saw an 86GT (4 spd.) being advertised for sale ($900). I drove 80 miles (one way) in about one hour and inspected inspected her. Everything was running ok, only needed a muffler. I paid for her then and there and she has been my baby since. Back then I did not know that such a car existed (only lived in this country since 1994&lt;&lt; originally from Jamaica, West Indies&gt;&gt; ). The mods. I have done so far... free flow cat. and muffler (ocelot), K&N filter, 160 stat and fan switch, MSD 6a ignition and coil, hypertech street runner chip and 88 5spd. tranny and shortened shifter (courtesy of "TOM" from this forum). It feels wonderful to see all you guys/gals making the effort to preserve this unique sports car, which I think was, and still is "AHEAD OF IT'S TIME."||||reg||6
Z||000009||Greg Piet||09-17-1999||02:30 AM||||I saw the first fiero in 84 at the ripe old age of 11, and told my mom (I want one of those).<BR>Over the years I had a few friends that had them, and I fell in love with a lady friends 86 v6 auto.  It was fast as hell.  I then herad of the potential for a V8, and I made the plunge.  I have had quite a few problems, but I dont regret it at all!<BR>By the way I am a huge fan of having the engine behind the driver, so with the current  production of vehicles Im pretty sure my next car will be another fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DaRkLoRD||09-17-1999||02:33 AM||||When I was about 11, a book I read mentioned a Fiero. One of the characters owned one, and I asked my dad if I could get a Fiero when I was older. He said I probably could. Later that summer, I finally saw one for real down in the USA.. That's when I definitely knew I wanted one. It's 8 years later, and I still can't afford one, but I'm learning as much about them as I can so I'll know what to do with it when I do get one.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||RossT||09-17-1999||02:39 AM||||About 2 years ago, I sold off all my old<BR>Mopars. It was going to cost to much to<BR>keep them running. Then about 6 months<BR>ago I got the bug for another play toy. Since muscle care have become outragous in price, I started to look for <BR>an alternative.  Back in the 70's my uncle<BR>had the coolest car (1970 Opel GT). I thought that that might be my new toy. After looking around on the net, I became<BR>frustrated with the quality of the cars for<BR>sale.  Everything needed lots of work.  My new toy was going to be a weekend driver only, and I wanted something nice  that would turn heads, and maybee increase in<BR>value. My nephews best friend is building<BR>a lambergini (sp?) from a 87 gt with 350 chevy nitrous.  He said I should look at the<BR>fiero.  I laughed, until I went to the internet.<BR>I could not believe the number of fiero sites<BR>out there.  We used to laugh about these<BR>cars back in the 80's.  But you know, the<BR>cars that people laugh about are usually<BR>the ones that become collectable.<BR>   So my search began! At first I thought that I had to have the rarest one, 88gt t-top. I quickly found that the rest of the<BR>country also wanted the 88gt t-top.  If one<BR>came up for sale, at a good price, it would<BR>be gone by the time you called the owner.<BR>This happend to me twice. Then something happend.  I saw a little add for<BR>an 88gt sunroof car for sale in Michigan.<BR>" under 500 miles and faithfully maintained"<BR>I thought that it was a misprint.  The car<BR>most likely had 5,000 miles on it.  The guy was asking, what I thought was alot of money, $12,900!!! But I called anyway. Turns out that the guy had worked for Pontiac back in the 80's and saw the end<BR>of the Fiero coming. Thinking that it would<BR>be a good investment, he bought one, and<BR>stuck it under a cover in his gararge. He would start it from time to time and drive it<BR>around the block. It was twice the amount<BR>of money that I wanted to pay, but this<BR>was my chance to have one of those<BR>show cars that everyone dreams about.<BR>I looked at it this way. " you cann't buy<BR>a 500 mile new sports car for that kind<BR>of money" I couldn't go wrong. Thus I<BR>was off to Michigan to tow home my new<BR>toy. The best part of the deal was when I was ready to leave for home the guy comes out with a suitcase full of Fiero related stuff. (orginal window sticker,build sheet,88 press guide,fiero jacket, 88 gm service manual, fiero at watkins glen  ramed picture, all kinds of pontiac fiero memo's sent to pontiac executives in 1988,etc.etc...) Needless to say I had<BR>a rather large grin on my face, for a long<BR>long time..........People around here (North Dakota) think I am nuts or buying it.  But I don't care.  That smile is still on my face..<BR>No regrets......  Ross<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000012||Patrick||09-17-1999||03:13 AM||||<BR>To be quite honest, I finally bought a Fiero after 23 years of owning other cars because I was so tired of seeing all my once beautiful Chevies turning into rust buckets. Every car I ever owned prior to this 2.5L 5-speed ‘87 coupe was a high performance V8 powered Chev. I still have a ‘78 Z28 Camaro, but it's dissolving into the ground in the back yard while I decide what to do with it.<P>The other choice I could have made for a rust free car would have been a <B>Saturn</B>, but they don't do a thing for me. Don't get me wrong, I enjoy my Fiero, I love the gas mileage, but being from the "old" school, I don't like having to deal with sensors and ECMs. It's a fun car, but I kinda miss the neck snapping power of my previous vehicles. I've never considered getting a V6 Fiero because, well, V6s don't do anything for me either. Nothing sounds like a V8, especially one that has been poked and prodded a bit. Yes, I'm a little envious of the fellas at the forum here who have V8 powered Fieros.<P>PS. (I've added this after reviewing my post.) Another choice for a rust free car could have been a <B>Corvette</B>, but besides the high price for them, I always felt that they were impractical. Only two seats and limited (if any) storage space. Well, the Fiero may be cheaper, but hardly any more practical.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Patrick (edited 09-18-1999).]||||reg||6
Z||000013||Cliff Pennock||09-17-1999||04:46 AM||||During a holiday in Pittsburgh back in '86, I saw a Fiero for the first time. It was a red GT V6 Fastback and I instantly fell in love with it (I still have the picture my sister took of me and that car). I decided there and then that was the car I wanted. At that time I couldn't afford buying one and shipping it to Holland (import tax alone was around $10,000). Three years later when my job required me to stay in San Antonio for about a year I bought my first Fiero: a black 85 GT V6 Notchback. I brought it back to Holland with me.<P>Before that time, I was never really fond about sportscars. I didn't care for Porches (still don't) and the ones I liked where way out of my league (Ferrari's, Lamborghini's). Besides, the Fiero is still one of the most beautiful cars ever made in my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000014||DJRice||09-17-1999||08:46 AM||||Actually, My dad started my Fiero interest.  He read and article in a magazine not long after the Fiero was introduced and knew right away he had to have one.  It wasnt until about 2 years ago that we found our first, an 84 2m4 that was in very good shape, but had a knock in the engine.  We picked it up for about $300.  After a motor change it was on the road and saving me loads of gas money driving back and forth to college and work.<P>My dad drives the silver 84 2m4, My brother drives an 86 2m6, and I drive an 87 v6.  <P>Basically, I dont think that any other <B>American</B> car has as much class as the Fiero does.  <P>BTW, Saturns were mentioned as GMs other rust free car.  Lessons learned from Fiero helped Saturn become what it is today.||||reg||12
Z||000015||ChadMan||09-17-1999||09:20 AM||||I fell in love with the Fiero at Mid-Ohio Sports Car Course, at an IMSA race in 1984.  The Dole and Son-Of-A-Gun Fieros were racing there, and Pontiac had a tent set up in the infield with Fieros.  I was 13 years old.  But as the car mags started writing negative crap about Fieros, I believed it, and stopped dreaming of owning one.<P>Fast-forward to 1992, when I was going to Ohio State and in need of good sporty, yet inexpensive transportation.  I was about to buy a CRX when I saw this gorgeous little red 84 Coupe in a guy's front yard.  When I drove it, I fell head-over-heels in love with it.  I have owned at least one Fiero ever since.  As the Minnesota club guys say, "FIEROS FOREVER!"||||reg||6
Z||000016||Dave85||09-17-1999||10:18 AM||||I have always loved the Fiero since they came out but never bought one. Then a few years ago the kit cars started to improve and that was the thing. I finally bought my first Fiero to build a Lambo. in May of this year. Well, I fell in love with this car and wouldn't dream of changing it now. I tell people all the time that I will never be without a Fiero!! ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Dave85||09-17-1999||10:20 AM||||BTW I have an '85 GT in Black.||||reg||1
Z||000018||bHooper||09-17-1999||12:29 PM||||When the Fiero's first came out I thought they were cute.  I was in college when the Fastback GT's came out, I thought they were beautifull cars.  In '89 my buddy bought a 86.5 GT and I fell in Love with a car for the first time.  I bought my 86.5 Gt November of '89 for $7500, a great price for a car that was in perfect shape.  After 10 years I still have the car, although it now needs a transmission a rebuild and a paint job.  Many people have felt pitty for me owning this car.  Let them enjoy their Taurus's and minivans......I'll keep my Fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  <P>By the way.....any transmissions for sale out there???????<p>[This message has been edited by bHooper (edited 09-17-1999).]||||reg||6
Z||000019||BENGAL4||09-17-1999||12:33 PM||||I saw my first Fiero in a shopping mall parking lot in 1984. A freind of mine purchased one a little later and all I could say was "WOW". I knew I had to have one.  I was driving a Ford EXP at the time and once Ihad ridden in the Fiero, I realized what a dog I owned. Years later my dream came true. I found a white 86GT 4 speed that was owned by an older getleman. He had to sell it because his son was turning 16 and his insurance was about to double in price. I looked at it and it was love at first sight.The purchase price was $4500.00. This was in 1993. She only had 40,000 miles on hert and was in great shape. She now has 157,000 miles on her and I have enjoyed almost every mile. Over the last year, I started restoring her and am almost finished. She has received the following.<P>New front fascia.<BR>New rocker panels.<BR>New left quarter window.<BR>New white paint. (Ground effects are white also.<BR>New carpet.<BR>Mr. Mikes leather seats.<BR>Homemade wood dash effects. (Ask Gokart.His low budget idea is great).<BR>Power pully.<BR>ADS chip.<BR>New cat back exhaust.<BR>New timing chain & gear set.<BR>American wheels.<P>I plan to put in a new engine within the next year. Maybe a GN 3.4 twin turbo. <BR>She is now driven only in the good weather months and not everyday. I made the mistake of selling another classic (1970 Mach 1) but Iwill keep my GT until I die. ||||reg||14
Z||000020||88 Fiero||09-17-1999||01:32 PM||||I've always been a GM freak and an avid lover of Pontiac Cars...specially the Trans-Am's...But when the Fiero came out in 1984..I totally lost control of myself and bought one...well now I have an '88 V6 and still a living freak...Oh...I think the reason why I love my car is because it's a Poorman's Ferrari....||||reg||10
Z||000021||JIM GOOSE||09-17-1999||02:05 PM|||| I always liked the FIERO for its unique qualities such as space frame & plastic body, of course the mid-engine.  I had owned a Lotus Europa twincam that was badly damaged at an SCCA event when a wildeyed fool ina Porsche ran into the backend.  After looking at lots of cars, I found an 88 GT 5spd and bought from orig. owner at reasonable price. Orig. intended to inst. a V-8 but chg. my mind & bgt. twin turbo kit which have yet to install.  Engine out and rebuilt with lots of mods for turbo compatibility which spent as much as did on car. Just got tired of working on it and spent too much up front to continue with turbo's. I got good buy on kit so couldnt pass it up and will ccomplete at some future time.  Been driving for some time now and recently repl. struts & shox,dogbone,rear tires etc. about 600$ worth.  Now and then, somebody will notice the car and comment about it, which is always nice.||||reg||12
Z||000022||FieroGT||09-17-1999||02:54 PM||||I remember when I bought my first Fiero. I was about 20 and my '78 Z28 was not long for this world. I was torn between an '84 RX7 and a black '85 SE coupe. Took the Fiero for a test drive and she died on me. Turns out the battery was dead. I came back with a new battery and left the guy a deposit. I have owned three other Fieros, as well. Currently, I still have the '85 SE and an '86 GT. In the 8 years I have owned the SE, it's received a V6-4spd combo, factory ground effects, spoiler, and full interior redo. The body is showing it's age, especially since it's been wrecked twice since I've owned it. I keep putting it back together and still (with 160,000 miles on it) drive the car to work every day, all year round. I don't think I'll ever be without a Fiero. ||||reg||1
Z||000023||mshill||09-17-1999||05:08 PM||||About a month ago my wife and I were out driving in my Ford Taurus, going to dinner and a movie, when she says "wouldn't it be nice to have a little sporty two seater like when we were first maried?"  <P>I had since my teens wanted to build a kit car and I started to investigate again.  Found out that the Fiero being a nid-engine V6 that it lent itself well to the European type replicas.  <P>A few weeks ago I bought one for $750 and am thinking about keeping my 86 SE for a daily driver and then getting another to put a ferrari Dino or Lambo Countach body on.<P>Love it.||||reg||1
Z||000024||David||09-17-1999||05:40 PM||||Unlike most replies here, I never really wanted a Fiero.  My brother bought an '88 Forumla, V6 5 sp, and wanted to keep it nice, so he bought this '86 4cyl 5sp that had a bad engine and a damaged left front corner to rebuild and use as his daily ride.  He also bought a '84 that had run so far under the back of a jacked-up pick-up that the truck's tires wouldn't allow the Fiero's doors to open.  He then decided that he had too many cars and projects and wanted to rid himself of the two older cars, keeping the '88.  I still wasn't interested but thought that this might be a good father-son project, so I purchased the two cars.  Also, thrown in was a '88 Olds balancer shaft engine that was in good condition.  The father-son part turned out to be a big bust, Brian really wasn't too interested.  I worked on getting the car running and was nearly complete when my then 17 year old daughter took my Cadillac for what was it's final ride.  She said it wasn't her fault, that it would have happened to whoever was driving at the time.  Anyways, I finished the Fiero and have 35,000 miles on it since I started driving it, total milage is about 92k miles.  I like the car, it has given me good service, and would like to have another someday for a project car.||||reg||1
Z||000025||cmyokley||09-17-1999||06:20 PM||||I had always rebuilt Corvetts, two years ago when my twin boys were 15 I decided to teach them how to rebuild cars so when they got stranded in a car they would know what to look for. I also was not going to build them a corvette and let them drive that to school.<P>I did want to build a sporty and unique car so I did the old net surf and found the Fiero. I had several friends when I was younger that had one and they enjoyed them very much so WE decided to find and rebuild a Fiero.<P>We found and purchased a 86GT in VERY poor condition. If I knew then what I know now I would have spent more and gotten a better project, and with more money an 88.<P>Well two years later and LOTS of money we have a very fine example of a Fiero. The car is beautiful, runs great and turns heads...what more could a 17 year want...well now the one that is interested in it the most wants a turbo. Oh well, he is following in the old mans love for speed.<P>The Fiero is a great car.||||reg||10
Z||000026||87GT||09-17-1999||08:57 PM||||Well my story goes like this.  I decided to go with a friend one year to the spring car show and swap at Daytona International Speedway in Florida.  It was the spring show in 1998.  We stayed at a Holiday Inn(I think)on the beach.  While at this hotel, I noticed that there were many many little cars at the hotel.  I saw some of the owners walking around with trophies. Of these little cars, some said, "2M4", some said "GT", and some said, "2M6."  I always knew of the Fiero but did not pay attention to them.  I just know I saw the GTs sitting around there and LOVED them.  The next morning when we were heading to the Speedway, these little Fieros were all heading there also.  It was such I site, I couldnt believe it.  Anyhow, I finally found a gorgeous 87GT 5-SP in immaculate shape here in the small town I live in.  I never saw it before, but now it is in my front yard!! 8-)  I purchased it in April of '99 and have yet to have a problem.  I need to go find some wood to knock on. lol!  8-)||||reg||7
Z||000027||Eric||09-17-1999||09:04 PM||||Up until a year ago, I never wanted a Fiero.  My dad came home one day and had some crazy idea of building a hotrod out of one of these little two seaters.  Of course I said okay, but I really didn't think I would ever own one.  He was deadset on getting an 88GT and left me to find one for a good price and in need of some work.  After months of searching I found a black one in Dallas (I live in Ohio).  I flew down there with my dad and tried not to act too interested (Its bad for negotiations when the seller knows you flew 1200 miles to look at his car).  Needless to say, we paid the asking price and started on the journey back to Ohio.  Honestly, I didn't like the car at first.  The handling was sloppy because of bad ball joints.  The paint was bad.  And I wasn't too fond of the black wheels on a black car (Still not.  I'm painting them gold).  I love the car now.  As planned, we are souping it up with a 3.1 rebuild and fixing the damage caused by 140,000 miles of hard driving.  Hopefully, the car will be ready to drive in less than a month.  It's been too long and I miss my Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Gokart||09-17-1999||09:05 PM||||Maybe I was blind to Fieros for many years, but the first time I got a good like at one was in 92. After my ex-wife left my son and I, we went from shitbox to shitbox(Colt, Datsun station wagon, etc.) I finally had enough money saved to buy a respectable looking car. A buddy told me about a Fiero he knows for sale for $1500.00. I had no idea what a Fiero looked like and more yet, that my life would change forever! After taking it for a test drive and it ran awesome, I thought for sure there had to be something wrong. How could this guy let this beautiful sports car, which was running great, go for $1500.00? Because I had no clue what a Fiero was, I thought I was getting something like a Ferrari or a Lamborghini! I ended up selling that 4-banger when I met my new wife so we could get our Plymouth Voyager in 95. Paying more attention to Fieros when I say them, I made up my mind someday I wanted a GT! Well my dream came true last April! If someone today wanted to trade me a Ferrari or a Lamborghini for my GT, I'd take it, sell it, a buy five more Fieros!||||reg||7
Z||000029||JetroGT||09-17-1999||10:04 PM||||I like the Fiero because it always looks good, handles alot better than most cars, and you don't see one on every street corner. <BR>	I bought my first Fiero from a friend. It was a gold 86 2M4 5spd. w/ a sun roof.  I drove it for about 4 years, and put about 60 some thousand miles on it, and a few thousand dollars into it to keep it running. Then I bought my first GT. It was an 86 with all the options including the performance sound. I had it for a little over a year, blew the engin, crashed it on the interstate, then the engine caught on fire because the people that replaced the engine forgot to replace the O-rings on the fuel rail where the cold start injector screws in. Then I got it fixed $$$, and then sold it for $1800.00, just enough to pay off the loan. Then after driving an 88 Nissan sentra for almost a year I bought another Fiero GT to restore. It turned out to be a nightmare. Now it's my parts car. Soon after that I bought the car I drive to work which is my 86 SE. Soon to be GT.  Everyone asks me why I keep spending more money on them d@m Fieros. 			                           																	<BR>	It's because I can't stand driving any thing else.||||reg||1
Z||000030||BlackGT||09-20-1999||01:19 PM||||2 years ago I was shopping for an 86-90 RX-7<BR>My best friend had just bought an 87 GT from some rich guy who used the car on cross country trips cause it felt good on the open road. The guy wanted to make sure that the car would go to some one who would take care<BR>and enjoy "her", he felt my buddy would make <BR>a fine new owner so he sold it to him for $1800. It had 130K miles when he got it and he drove it to hell and back for about 2 months before I came to visit him. I told him it was a nice car but I was set on buying an RX-7 he said before you do that take this car for a spin.....I had intended to  take the car for a quick spin around the block but<BR>after 3 hours I came back and my buddy had this dumb shit eatin grin on his face and said " Ya still want one of them rx-7`s? "<BR>I`ve been hooked ever since. I just couldn`t<BR>find one like his until recently. I bought myself an 86GT and drove it for 500 miles before the engine let go! I cried for a while<BR>and found a good mechanic who not only works <BR>on anything but also likes FIEROs too. Right <BR>now he is doing an engine swap for me with a <BR>new rebuilt 2.8 and does not want me to rush<BR>him so he needs a couple of weeks to make sure every things perfect when I get it back!<BR>I think he just wants to spend some time behind the wheel listening to that exhaust note!!!||||reg||6
Z||000031||Formula||09-20-1999||08:30 PM||||I was 17 when I bought a 1987 ford escort exp.  After spending 2 months behind the wheel driving it. I decided that I needed a more reliable (more powerful) car. SO, I sold the EXP for $1300 and saved my min. wage job money until I had $2000. I was going to go to the auction with my uncle to find a Cavalier Z24 or a 4 cyl Ford probe (newer style).  I never made it to the auction.  I went to a local dealer one night and saw a beautiful 2 seat car in the lot. well, I figured it was way out of my price range. BUT, I went back the next day to ask the price and to my surprise it was only $1500.  What kind I say, I wrote a check and forgot about the Z24 completly.  I am a certified Fiero NUTCASE||||reg||1
Z||000032||Standard||09-20-1999||11:59 PM||||A little over a year ago I was searching for my first car.. I wanted a late 80's firebird/camero v8, but (of course) my parents said no way.  So off I was to the used car dealer to look at all the boring 4 doors that would keep the insurance prices down.. then I saw this little red car that said 2m4 on the back, and I was hooked.  I got on the internet, found out everything I could about the car.. printed out the reports about how safe the thing was to show to my parents.. when I went to test drive it I knew it was going to be mine.  I love having a $2,000 dollar car that people stare at because it looks so unique!  I like the uniqueness of it.. I can't wait till I get my GT...||||reg||6
Z||000033||Racer 1||09-21-1999||03:48 AM||||When I was first introduced to my 86 coupe fiero was at a autolot. Man did this car look way sweet. Took me about 3 hours of knee begging and constant prayers to finally convince my dad I needed this car. Just the looks made me drewl and thinking of all the lovely young ladies I was gonna pick up in this sports car. Constant begging and promising to do good in school and to do chores got me my fiero. First drive of the parking lot to my house was like a dream. When I got home I found that the air inlet piece had taken a hike. First thing out was the ford stereo that came with it. In went the big stereo system. Went thru hell with this car and its problems. But I have hung in there and love my fiero almost as much as my family. Now my fiero sits in my garage I get to drive it about once a week and just put in a "SWEET" new custom exhaust system. My car has 206,000 mile on the OEM iron duck 4 banger. Cant wait till that baby goes and the Qaud-4HO comes in!!<p>[This message has been edited by Racer 1 (edited 09-21-1999).]||||reg||10
Z||000034||Pat||09-23-1999||03:19 AM||||They make great cars.<BR>All they fit is your ass and 10.5 gallons of gas.<BR>But seriously folks...I bought it for my girlfriend and sometimes I let her drive it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000035||Richard Parnell||09-23-1999||04:21 PM||||I first started noticing Fieros about 4-5 years ago.  I'd see ads in the paper for them and couldn't believe how cheap they were, so I looked around at a few.  My intention (or so I told my wife) was to by one fix it up a little and sell at a profit. I finally went and checked out an 87 SC (gold).  The guy wanted 1500 for it, I offered 1000 and he jumped on it.  He seemed to be in a hurry to get out of town.  He been in a wreck with it.  Front and rear fascias are cracked and as a result the seam along the front of the hood is slightly askew.  Haven't replaced the parts yet, but still looks good.  Anyway, I didn't really like the car too much at first, handled ok but since I love to golf I always keep my clubs in the car in case someone says the magic word.  I didn't like the clubs banging back and forth in the front seat until one day I tried to put them in the small trunk space behind the engine.  They fit and the rest is history.  I will never part with that car. I plan on putting a V8 in it ((Carbureted, I Hate computerized cars)sorry purists), and fixing suspension, making it pretty etc.  I figure even if I spend 10,000 on it, it will still be cheaper than buying some new piece of computerized junk and I know how to fix everything practically on the car. What a deal!||||reg||12
Z||000036||fierosound||11-26-1999||02:29 PM||||I've always builtup and maintained my cars (V8 performance usually) beautifully, only to have it be dissolved by rust no matter what I did (other than NOT DRIVE it at all).  What's the point then!!<P>My last car was a 1979 Chev Monza Spyder with 350hp 350 V8 4-speed (was a 305 factory V8 originally).  This was also a GM underdog/orphan built only for 4 years. It too starting getting the disease, and I started looking for another car.(1989)<P>One day I saw a sharp racy looking Burgundy/Grey 2 seater in a parking lot - had no idea what it was.  PONTIAC was in the rear light lenses with a small GT in the black trim.  I went to a Pontiac dealer to ask what it was. I then found out it was a 87 FIERO GT, and that Fieros were discontinued.  I found out everything I could about the car.  PLASTIC BODY MID-ENGINE sold me.<P>10 months later (Dec 1989) I found a car that looked exactly the same, loaded (except air), 27,250km (17,300 miles), summer driver, never been smoked in etc.  Virtually a new, just broken-in car for $9750CDN  A much better deal than a new $32,000CDN MR2!!<P>I've had the car ever since.  Did a few performance mods (chip, K&N, ported manifolds etc) and added a high-end stereo system ( <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierosound" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fierosound</A>  ).<P>I'm now approaching 160,000km (100,000 miles). Still runs great, but upgrading to 3.4L performance engine and new paint in spring 2000.<P>FIEROs - the biggest secret is "they're alot better than most people think".<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000037||Voyd||11-26-1999||03:42 PM||||ever since i was a kid and first spotted a Fiero, i knew that i wanted one and when came time to purchase my own car, it would be a Fiero. <P>anytime i saw a Fiero on the street i would say "fiero" out loud and point to it to the person(s) next to me. they thought it was funny and that the dream would fade. as years past and the fieros slowly began disapearing from the road my dream still held on very tight. all thru my teens when id see a Fiero id still point it out to anyone i was with. i think every one of my friends knew that i was nuts about thhis car and that i would end up owning one.<P>now at 20 yrs old, it came time for me torealize my dream. since last month im now the proud owner of a Black 85 SE   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P><p>[This message has been edited by Voyd (edited 11-26-1999).]||||reg||10
Z||000038||mutfish||11-26-1999||03:55 PM||||I have always liked the looks of the Fiero,but have had a corvette project in the garage for the last few years and didn't give much more than a passing thought to buying one.A couple of years ago I read an article in one of the corvette magazines written buy a collector car broker(Mike Katain I think is his name)recmmending low mileage '88 GT's as a good investment.I knew of a nice '88 for sale not far from me,so I eventually bought it. As far as a good investment, we'll have to wait and see.In the meantime I've become very attached to this little car.||||reg||1
Z||000039||overlord||11-26-1999||07:35 PM||||i first bought my fiero when i wrecked my car and needed transportation home...i seen it in the auto trader and decided to check it out....when i got there it was sitting on the driveway in the sun all nice and polished and i said to myself "no way, that cant be it" and i was hooked.<P>im glad i bought it cause i love that car..||||reg||1
Z||000040||2M4 Dale||11-26-1999||11:04 PM||||Last winter I was working in Cleveland and I Live in Mansfield. Thats a two hour + drive. For the winter weather my 4WD Suburban was great. As the weather got better I needed something a bit more economical to drive. I didn't want a econo-box so I started looking for something sporty with a four cylinder. (gas Mileage) When I found my 87 coupe I knew that was the car I wanted. It needed some mechanical work but nothing major. About two weeks after I got the Fiero I quit my job in Cleveland. I now work about 7 minutes from home...I still have the Fiero. I plan on having it for a very long time!!!!!||||reg||14
Z||000041||loafer87gt||11-26-1999||11:34 PM||||I can remember when I was 18 and looking for my first car. I was looking for a an import car at the time, mainly because that is what my family and myself had driven previously. I was getting very depressed as all the nice cars I found were way out of my price league, and seemed to be lacking in character. Then one day while I was flipping through an autotrader I found an 87 Fiero GT. I instantly fell in love with it. I called the owner and he said the car had sold. I was in such a slump. A couple days later, the guy called back, and told me the kid coulnd't get the loan to get the car, so it was still for sale. Me and my Dad went to take a look at it and it was in mint shape. I let my Dad drive it out of the city for me (I'm a country boy) and you could tell he was enjoying himself. He said it reminded him of his old Fiat, and said even though the car may require more maintenance than others, that he can understand why I liked the car so much. Now five years later, I still walk backwards when walking away from my car so I can admire its great looks. To me, happiness is blasting down the twisty roads during summer with the roof off wound out in third.  Thanks for the excitement Pontiac!||||reg||1
Z||000042||87GT||11-27-1999||12:03 AM||||I read some of the new posts here and noticed many similarites between my love of fieros and everyone elses.. I too, yell out Fiero when I see one going down the road.  Another thing I do is walk backwards from my car as I go inside the house or wherever I am going.  From my place of work, I always go to the window to stair out at my car. LOL!!  ||||reg||1
Z||000043||Cliff D.||11-27-1999||05:00 AM||||I always wanted to build a race car, but I could not figure out what model. Last winter my friend from work started his '85 GT to warm it up and I just thought "that sounds great, what the hell is in that thing?" When he told me Multi-port injection on a Chevy V6 with manual steering, 4-wheel disk brakes and independent rear suspension my search was over right then. Lucky for me, 6 months later his clutch went out and he let me have it for $600.00!! About 2 months after that I found one exactly like it. I put that one on the road and my '98 firebird has been for sale ever since.||||reg||1
Z||000044||William Federle||11-27-1999||10:43 AM||||I bought my 1984 SE because of all of the<BR>used cars I saw for &lt; $700.00, it was the<BR>sportiest. I know that they can be had for<BR>less, but it doesn't emit great clouds of<BR>smoke when it runs, there were no puddles of<BR>oil under it and when I test drove it it was<BR>fun to drive. I've had to replace a lot of<BR>sensors (and except for the manifold pressure<BR>sensor, they all yielded increases in drivability.) The fun started to wear off when I had to replace the clutch master cylinder and pedal, but the car suckered me<BR>in and I think I'll just keep it. I drive it<BR>100 miles /day to and from work.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000045||fieroshop||11-27-1999||10:55 AM||||Well when the first Fiero came out I was an assistant sales manager for a Pontiac dealer. I liked the car but to me it was a car that I could sell and make good money. I 1984 I quit the out industry and did not think about Fiero. Some years later (1992) I was unable to work and needed a car. Since I,ve always had and loved sportcars I started to look for one. One day I saw a red 84 Fiero for sale at a used car dealer. I bought the car and drove it for three years until somebody stole my pride and joy. During the three years I knew that I wanted an 88GT. I needed a car to drive and so I bought a camaro. I still wanted a Fiero and a year later I found my 88GT.(1996) I will not sell this car at any price. It runs and looks like new and I intend on keeping it this way. Since then I have opened up an exclusive Fiero Shop in Hayward and now own three parts cars and picking two more very shortly. I am also in the process of restoring an 86 SE and an 88 Cpe. These cars will be used as rentals for my customers. I am also picking up an 86 GT, 85 GT. These cars will be for sale. I just fine=ished selling a restored 86SE red auto.to a gal who is also joining oour club here in the San Francisco Bay Area. I guess you could say I've been bitten by the Fiero bug.||||reg||6
Z||000046||Wolfhound||11-27-1999||03:26 PM||||I liked the Fiero from the first time I read about it and saw photos in 83. I had owned a 63 Corvette and a 68 Alfa Romeo GT. In 87 I was offered the use of an 86 SE 4 cylinder for the weekend to drive from Huntsville , where I was working, to Auburn, where I lived. Loved the way the car drove and wanted one from that moment on.<BR> Like to have never found how to open the gas cap on that trip, but that's another story and probably not uncommon.<BR>At the time they were still relatively expensive, even used. about 3 years ago I happen on a 84 base coupe for sale at a house I was working on. Traded the work for the Car and thing haven,t been the same round here since.<BR> I now have Eleven and just can,t help it. I,m still amazed that the value is so depressed for this car. I have developed the ability to spot them in peoples back yards with grass growing around them. I,ve got them for a (you can have it if you will get it out of my yard) to my latest 87 Blue GT 5 Speed for $1800. It helps that I like messing with cars. It handles almost as good as the Alfa and way better than the Corvette. <BR>Incedently now own the 86SE that I first drove. <BR>I could go on and on.<BR>    <p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 11-27-1999).]||||reg||7
Z||000047||hefty||11-27-1999||06:59 PM||||I bought my '87 GT in Sept. I sold my Samurai and was looking for a sporty 2 seater or truck. Not finding anything I could see myself driving, my boss asked if I was interested in his GT. He has a nice collection of cars and the GT had been in a heated barn with a couple other cars for several years. I jumped all over it without even driving it ! I love everything about the car.||||reg||1
Z||000048||2birds||11-27-1999||09:12 PM||||I bought my 86 GT back in 1988.  I was 20, and I was looking for a 5.0 Mustang.  I had grown up on hot rods (I learned how to drive in a 33 Terraplane street rod and I drove a 390 powered 52 Ford pickup until I finished high school), but all of the Mustangs I found turned out to be more expensive than I was willing to pay.  I found my Fiero at a used car dealer in Hanover, Pa.  I decided to test drive it, and I was hooked from the start.  I paid $8600 back then, for a 16,000 mile 86 Fastback GT, 5-speed, air, sunroof, power windows, red/silver, with wing.  A new 5.0 LX was selling for $13,000 and up, with 1-2 year old Mustang GT's selling for even more, so I got the Fiero instead.  It was different, so it fit.  I finally got the Ford void filled with my other hobby, Mn-12 Thunderbirds.  Seems all the cars I like get cancelled when they reach perfection...      ||||reg||1
Z||000049||tabasco||11-28-1999||05:06 PM||||WHAT OTHER SPORTS CAR CAN YOU BY ? FOR AROUND 2.000 AND YOUR INC. WONT GO UP. IT A GREAT LOOKING CAR.AND WILL KEEP WITH THE BIG BOYS TO. EAT YOUR HEART OUT BIG BOYS! SE.V6 RED.LOOK OUT TRANS AM.||||reg||10
Z||000050||sdm1964||11-28-1999||06:59 PM||||I saw my first fiero when i worked at a pontiac dealer. New 84 Red coupe rolling off the car carrier, needless to say the initial inspection and test drive took a little longer than usual.  If you ever bought yours  new in central Florida i might have driven it before you!!!!!  I was 19 married with children. Now way i could ever afford one or talk the wife into buying one. Well the next year the Gt's came out and that was it. One day i would own one of these little rockets. Well like my other brothers in arms, i have owned the mini vans, wagons and crap second cars. Camaro's and vettes were always the dream cars i wanted to own. Two years ago i bought my 4th camaro Z28 for $500.00 and spent the next 2 years repairing and restoring it. I finally got it painted this summer enjoying the t-tops when a kid pulled out infront of me. It really did a number on the waxed once front end.It came to a decision, fix the Z, keep the insurance money and drive my wifes sunbird, she also has a van, or buy a new toy. This was the 3dr time the Z has been hit, My dad sayed it was an accident magnet, get rid of it. After getting the insurance check i had a hard time deciding. i was looking thru the auto trader and was a beautiful White 85 GT and remembered the days at the dealership. I told my wife i would sell camaro and get the GT. Well I am in the National Guard and she bought the car for me while i was out of town for drill. When i got back she sayed i needed to call the guy i bought the Z from because we was the original owner and wanted to buy it back from me. I never called him. Too much sweat put into that car. I will fix it again. But i have way too much fun driving this 85 and love the looks and power from the V-6. I have to look back at it when i walk back into the house. I will be willing to sell the Z28 to get me an 86-88 GT fastback, Red. I really love these cars, cheap, fun to drive and not one of those smooth buble cars we have now. DRIVE ON BROTHER IN PLASTIC!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!<BR>ps, check mine out Steve Mein <A HREF="http://www.intap.net/~bozone/fiero/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.intap.net/~bozone/fiero/</A> <BR>||||reg||14
Z||000051||Raydar||11-28-1999||08:20 PM||||When the Fiero was first introduced, I thought it was "cute", but I didn't get too excited. I was also one of those "if 'more' is enough, then 'too much' is just right" guys. Was driving a 76 Monza 2+2 with a '70 350/300 motor, and a 4 speed. I just HAD to hold out for the (rumored) V-6. When Pontiac offered the V-6 in 85, and THEN wrapped it in the Indy bodywork, I was toast. Went out and bought a new 85 GT. First new car I'd ever bought. Ended up trading it in on an 88 Firebird Formula, because I needed more room. (Did this two weeks before they announced the cancellation of the Fiero. My dumb ass.) Bought an 88 Fiero coupe in December of 97. Then, in March of 99, I sold the Firebird (the biggest GM P.O.S. that I have ever seen), and bought the 88 Formula T-top. My wife thinks I'm obsessed, and... maybe she's right!  ||||reg||1
Z||000052||Scott2M4||11-29-1999||11:30 AM||||Top ten reasons why I bought my Fiero:<P>10. Plastic body - no rust!<BR> 9. Corners like it's on rails!<BR> 8. Low insurance costs!<BR> 7. Inexpensive in purchase price!<BR> 6. Parts are easy to find and cheap to buy!<BR> 5. Much more reliable and better daily driver than a comparable X1/9 or TR7!<BR> 4. One hell of a following and support group (Fiero Forum Subscribers, stand up and give yourself a round of applause!)!<BR> 3. I look   G O O D   in it!<BR> 2. One of the best-looking cars to come out of GM in the past 20 years!<P>and, the number one reason for buying my Fiero:<P> 1. "Rare" - not as common as the CRX, MR2 and RX-7!||||reg||1
Z||000053||Bugsy||12-01-1999||09:13 AM||||My mom started my fascination with the Fiero, back in '88 she was looking for something sporty and sexy...the middle age crisis I think...but she'd always driven full sized GM sports cars....she'd just totaled her '65 Impala SS. Anyway..I insist on accompanying here when she's shopping for cars.  She found a nice 86GT and fell in love with it. I forbid it. I told her it was a death trap and i didn't want to bury her before I was 100...LOL, anyway, I refused to even let her drive the Fiero. She ended up with an 86 Camaro 5.0 fully equipped with 17k miles on it....About 7 years ago, my boss, a rather LARGE lady, traded her Towncar in on an 86SE, one of my responsibilities was to keep her car clean..normal maint...check the oil, crap like that....I didn't want to even be seen in the car....till I drove it. The rest is history I guess. 5 years ago I started looking for an 86SE just like hers.. I couldn't find one in decent condition for a decent price...Back in May I blew the tranny in my Park Avenue and spun a bearing getting it off the street...now what??. I'd just sold my second car a month before. So I started hitting the local used car lots...looking at Sevilles, Marks, a couple of 4x4's, I couldn't find anything that really impressed me till i drove by a small local lot, it was a red '86 SE V-6 4-speed, sunroof, the only blemish on the paint was the spoiler, it had faded badly. It had 72k miles on it. never been smoked in. This car was 3 rows back from the front line, it just pulled me in like we were meant to be together...I spent 2 days with the car before I even took it off the lot for a test drive. The car made a liar out of me...I swore I'd never own a red car (I live in Nebraska, and I'm NOT a cornhusker fan)...anyway needless to say, the dealer was probably glad to get rid of me..after 3 days of crawling under it, thru it I even took a mechanics stethescope to the engine and tranny, anyway, I bought the car, the throw out bearing is making a little noise, and I've discovered that I'm having to add fluid to the clutch resevoir...can't find the leak....yet...lol. The real suprise was that my insurance actually went down from my Park Avenue. I'd only had it 3 weeks when a colege student negotiated a left turn from the right lane...I broadsided his Rodeo..his left rear tire literally rolled over the passenger side of the front end...my baby was totaled and I hadn't even made the first payment. I didn't have full coverage and altho the other driver got a negligent driving ticket his insurance company STATE FARM is denying my claim...I'm suing of course. Anyway, after leaving it in the impound lot for a week, I couldn't take it anymore...LOL  I had it towed home, put it  on a trailer and got my required estimates...then to the frame shop, the tire shop, back to the frame shop, and I finally got it back. I rigged a headlight up so I had both working and am driving it with the nose and passenger fender bandaged together with 100 MPH racing tape...duct tape...LOL. I've managed to find and purchase all the needed parts to do the chassis repairs and replace the damaged body parts...it took me 3 months to find an SE nose in decent condition. I wouldn't have gone this far for any other car, and now i'm looking at an '88 GT sitting  on a local lot. Oh yea my mom is livid!!! She won't even ride in it...LOL. Her loss I guess.:||||reg||6
Z||000054||ratt||12-01-1999||03:49 PM||||My first Fiero was an 85 2m4/5spd.  I bought it because I needed a car that had better gas milage (I own a 67 firebird & a 83 T/A).  It rang up 300,000km before my girlfriend (now wife) parked it under an extended cab 4x4 (ouch!).  She then went and bought an 85se 2.8l for herself to drive because she liked how it got around in the snow.  I eventually replaced my totaled 85 with an 86se 2.8l.  I've modified both cars the same ( k&n filter, holley side scoop, accel super coil, & rear sway bar (just took a fiero front sway bar & mounted it on the rear - works great & really easy to do). Anyways, she still kicks my butt when we drag up the local highway (*sigh*).||||reg||1
Z||000055||kel||12-01-1999||09:28 PM||||Oh no!!!  Did someone mention duct tape???  Quick Cliff...before the bandit sees it...delete that message!!!!!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>and if you newbies don't know what I'm talking about, go here:   <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/000245.html" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/000245.html</A>  ||||reg||10
Z||000056||kel||12-01-1999||09:41 PM||||Come to think of it, I haven't seen him on here in awhile...hmmm....||||reg||1
Z||000057||Shiner||12-02-1999||02:50 AM||||I was looking for a first car, and went to see a red '84 RX7 GSL 5speed.  The car basicly sold the car out from under me.  So I went looking for another car.  I found my '84 SC auto.  The paint was (and still is) rough on the flat areas, but I fell in love with that car. The mid-engine layout really got me excited.  It was so great to drive the car and hear that engine humming 6 inches from my head.  I kept driving that one until I found my new one, an '87 GT 5speed.  That one was love at first sight too.  It felt like I was driving a compleatly different car.  This one is a total cosmetic restore project.  It runs good, and has a new clutch, so new red paint, spoiler, and interior is going to make this  a realy head turner.  I love fieros to death, and I love educating those people that say "oh, aren't those the cars that burn up" and "oh, that thing HAS to be a death trap"||||reg||1
Z||000058||Rob||12-02-1999||06:34 PM|||| About six years ago I sold my 67 Dodge 440 Six Pack and wanted a fast, fun car to drive that didn't break every time I drove it. About a week later I found an 85GT for sale for $2,800. Since that time I have modified it alot and still love working on it and most of all driving it.   ||||reg||1
Z||000059||fierosound||01-26-2000||09:10 PM||||I came across this on a Fiero owners website:<P><BR>Why you should buy a fiero, and why I did.<P>1) Couldn't afford a used 'vette.<P>2) Couldn't afford a new Chevette.<P>3) Couldn't afford insurance on either.<P>4) Fiero GT's out perform foreign cars at 2x the price.<P>5) Independent suspension.<P>6) Mid-Engine.<P>7) Looks cool!<P>8) Disc Brakes.<P>9) Safe as a volvo.<P>10) Hauls A$$.<P><BR>I pretty much agree with everything he said (but I don't especially like 'vettes or Chevettes)!!!!<P><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 01-26-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000060||Ed||01-26-2000||10:38 PM||||Anyone else here have one since they came out?  I bought mine in January of 1984. Somebody at work already had one by then. I looked at the Nissan 300Z and Mazda Rx7. They were a little more money than I wanted to pay.  The Fiero seemed just right. <P>My '84 was totaled a few years ago: What to replace it with? I realized that I liked my Fiero so much I'd get another, but MORE POWER!  I now have an '87 GT. <P>So, I've been a Fiero owner for 15 years. ||||reg||1
Z||000061||mshill||01-27-2000||12:56 AM||||Ed, I had an RX7.  The Fiero is faster and handles a lot better.||||reg||1
Z||000062||william_thorniii||01-27-2000||09:26 AM||||And a wankel is a @$%@$# to rebuild!<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000063||Monkeyman||01-27-2000||04:31 PM||||kel--I don't really consider myself a newbie but that thread was still before my time.  (Nice to have you back with us.  What happened?)  Maybe I'm more of a new oldbie.  Or an old newbie?  Maybe a midnewoldmidlateteensearly20slate30s<BR>What was the question?||||reg||1
Z||000064||lowCG||01-27-2000||05:13 PM||||I love the American build and inconspicous character,compared to foreign cars like the Alfa,Lancia,Opels,and Mini-Cooper I'd owned in the past.Now that I've gotten the finesse of the handling in order with a big pile of components from other vehicles,I just enjoy driving it everywhere.Nothing else drives like it,and is dent resistant also.||||reg||1
